ctrinated in a kind of thinking  and it happens over time and it happens  with peer pressure and separation from  everything that they're familiar with  and  you know we don't no one says i'm going  to join a cult  no one says that  but they can become enrapt in that  and controlled and do anything it is  that the leader tells them to do if they  believe it's in the greater good  were you surprised at the evidence  that's been presented in court in the  trial here in new york involving larry  ray and sarah lawrence students  i find it  you know i think i'm always surprised in  a sense  i'm always shocked  i'm always stunned  no matter what it is  and how i've heard it before and i think  sometimes one aspect of this  is that college students are smart  and  we have an idea that people join cults  who are not smart or maybe they're on  drugs or maybe they're out on the street  and homeless but the truth is  cult leaders want people who are smart  smart people will raise money for them  smart people will draw in more people  and recruit for them  and  so  in many respects  it  it's not surprising to see how this  could happen  in a college environment but it's still  stunning  what do you tell parents of students who  fall under the sway of these groups how  to recognize it and how to break the  hold  that's a really good question because  parents often do come to us and say i'm  worried about my son or daughter because  um they seem to be  meeting with this group that i don't  know much about don't understand and  their behaviors are changing  and  what we'd like to to do is talk about  what behaviors are you seeing  what kinds of things um concern you  and then the bottom line is to continue  communicating with your child no matter  what  arguing  about what they're  told  um and their belief system is not a way  to  uh  keep that commun communication tie open  so the important thing is to maintain  communication to be there no matter what  sometimes ask questions  